<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Objective</h2>

  <p>
    The purpose of this analysis is to quantify the minimum torsional stiffness
    required for the 2027 monocoque so that structural compliance does not
    significantly reduce the intended suspension response.
  </p>

  <p>
    The design target is for the overall vehicle stiffness to retain
    <strong>90%</strong> of the ideal stiffness set by the suspension spring
    parameters.
  </p>

  <p>
    The monocoque and suspension structures must therefore be sufficiently stiff
    that their combined compliance reduces the intended suspension response by no
    more than 10%.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Torsional Stiffness Definition</h2>

  <p>
    Torsional stiffness is defined as the applied torque divided by the resulting
    angular deflection:
  </p>

  \[
  K = \frac{T}{\theta}
  \]

  <p>where:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>\(K\) is torsional stiffness,</li>
    <li>\(T\) is applied torque,</li>
    <li>\(\theta\) is angular deflection.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For a torsional test in which a vertical force \(F\) is applied at a
    perpendicular lever arm \(L\):
  </p>

  \[
  T = FL
  \]

  <p>
    If the two sides of the structure experience a combined vertical displacement
    \(\Delta\), the twist angle can be approximated as:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta
  =
  \tan^{-1}
  \left(
  \frac{\Delta}{L}
  \right)
  \]

  <p>
    Therefore, the measured torsional stiffness is:
  </p>

  \[
  K
  =
  \frac{FL}
  {
  \tan^{-1}
  \left(
  \frac{\Delta}{L}
  \right)
  }
  \]

  <p>
    For sufficiently small angular deflections:
  </p>

  \[
  \tan^{-1}
  \left(
  \frac{\Delta}{L}
  \right)
  \approx
  \frac{\Delta}{L}
  \]

  <p>
    This gives the small-angle approximation:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K
  \approx
  \frac{FL^2}{\Delta}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    This expression directly relates the measured vertical deflection to the
    torsional stiffness of the structure.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Equivalent Torsional Wheel Rate</h2>

  <p>
    The physical suspension springs are linear springs, while the monocoque and
    suspension structural stiffnesses are expressed as torsional stiffnesses.
    The linear wheel rate must therefore be converted into an equivalent
    torsional stiffness.
  </p>

  <p>
    For one wheel located a distance \(L\) from the vehicle centerline:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{wheel,torsion}}
  \approx
  K_{\mathrm{wheel,linear}}L^2
  \]

  <p>
    This relationship assumes consistent units and an angular displacement
    measured in radians.
  </p>

  <h3>Motion Ratio Convention</h3>

  <p>
    Cornell Racing defines motion ratio as:
  </p>

  \[
  MR
  =
  \frac{
  \text{wheel displacement}
  }{
  \text{spring displacement}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    Using this convention, the installed linear wheel rate is:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{wheel,linear}}
  =
  \frac{
  K_{\mathrm{spring}}
  }{
  MR^2
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The torsional wheel rate selected from the 2026 suspension calculations was:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  =
  171.92\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    This represents the intended compliant behavior of the suspension springs
    during the one-wheel-bump loading case.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Series Stiffness Model</h2>

  <p>
    During a one-wheel-bump event, the suspension spring, suspension structure,
    and monocoque each contribute to the total angular displacement.
  </p>

  <p>
    When compliant components act in series, their angular deflections add:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta_{\mathrm{total}}
  =
  \theta_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  +
  \theta_{\mathrm{susp}}
  +
  \theta_{\mathrm{mono}}
  \]

  <p>
    Because:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta = \frac{T}{K}
  \]

  <p>
    the total vehicle stiffness is:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{vehicle}}}
  =
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    This equation shows that the softest element has the greatest influence on
    the overall vehicle stiffness.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>ARG26 Twist Test Data</h2>

  <p>
    Physical twist testing was used to determine the torsional stiffness of the
    ARG26 monocoque and the complete hub-to-hub chassis assembly. Two separate
    test configurations were required because they measure different parts of the
    vehicle.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first configuration isolates the composite monocoque as closely as
    possible. The second configuration applies the load through the wheel hubs,
    so the measured deformation includes both the monocoque and the suspension
    structure.
  </p>

  <figure class="engineering-diagram">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/Twist_test_setup.png' | relative_url }}"
      alt="Top view of the ARG26 vehicle showing the numbered twist-test measurement locations"
      class="img-fluid"
      loading="lazy"
    >

    <figcaption>
      ARG26 twist-test measurement locations. Points 5 and 6 are located at the
      front suspension attachment region of the monocoque and are used to measure
      the relative vertical displacement of the frame during the isolated
      monocoque test.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Monocoque-Only Test</h3>

  <p>
    To determine the monocoque stiffness, the load was introduced directly into
    the front suspension mounting region rather than through the complete
    suspension assembly. This minimizes deformation from the control arms,
    uprights, rockers, bearings, and other suspension components.
  </p>

  <p>
    A known vertical force was applied through a measured lever arm, producing
    an applied torque:
  </p>

  \[
  T = FL
  \]

  <p>
    where \(F\) is the applied vertical force and \(L\) is the perpendicular
    distance from the vehicle centerline to the load application point.
  </p>

  <p>
    The vertical movements at measurement locations 5 and 6 were recorded as
    the load was increased. Because one side moves upward relative to the other,
    the total relative displacement across the front of the monocoque is:
  </p>

  \[
  \Delta_{\mathrm{mono}}
  =
  \left|
  \Delta y_5-\Delta y_6
  \right|
  \]

  <p>
    Using the distance \(b_{\mathrm{mono}}\) between the two measurement
    locations, the corresponding monocoque twist angle is:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta_{\mathrm{mono}}
  =
  \tan^{-1}
  \left(
  \frac{\Delta_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  {b_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  \right)
  \]

  <p>
    For the small angular deflections observed during testing:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta_{\mathrm{mono}}
  \approx
  \frac{\Delta_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  {b_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  \]

  <p>
    Torque was then plotted against angular deflection. Since torsional stiffness
    is defined as:
  </p>

  \[
  K = \frac{T}{\theta}
  \]

  <p>
    the slope of the linear torque-versus-deflection relationship represents the
    torsional stiffness of the tested structure.
  </p>

  <figure class="engineering-diagram">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/Monocoque_stiffness_graph.png' | relative_url }}"
      alt="ARG26 monocoque torsional stiffness graph showing applied torque versus angular deflection"
      class="img-fluid"
      loading="lazy"
    >

    <figcaption>
      Monocoque-only test data. Each point represents an applied torque and the
      corresponding twist angle calculated from the relative displacement measured
      between locations 5 and 6. The slope of the fitted torque-deflection line is
      the monocoque torsional stiffness.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The finalized ARG26 monocoque test result used in this analysis was:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}
  =
  3364\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    This value represents the stiffness of the composite frame itself, including
    its bonded joints, inserts, local suspension mounting regions, and the global
    carbon-fiber sandwich structure.
  </p>

  <h3>Hub-to-Hub Test</h3>

  <p>
    The hub-to-hub test was then performed with the suspension structure installed.
    In this configuration, the torsional load is introduced at the wheel hub rather
    than directly into the monocoque.
  </p>

  <p>
    A known vertical force is applied at one hub while the remaining hub locations
    provide the test constraints. The applied torque is calculated from the hub
    force and the appropriate lateral lever arm:
  </p>

  \[
  T_{\mathrm{hub}} = F_{\mathrm{hub}}L_{\mathrm{hub}}
  \]

  <p>
    The relative vertical hub displacement is converted into a chassis twist angle:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta_{\mathrm{hub}}
  =
  \tan^{-1}
  \left(
  \frac{\Delta_{\mathrm{hub}}}
  {b_{\mathrm{hub}}}
  \right)
  \]

  <p>
    The resulting torque-versus-deflection plot includes deformation from both:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>the composite monocoque, and</li>
    <li>
      the suspension structure, including links, clevises, uprights, rockers,
      bearings, joints, and mounting interfaces.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <figure class="engineering-diagram">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/hub_to_hub_graph.png' | relative_url }}"
      alt="ARG26 hub-to-hub torsional stiffness graph showing torque versus hub deflection"
      class="img-fluid"
      loading="lazy"
    >

    <figcaption>
      Hub-to-hub test data. The slope is lower than the monocoque-only stiffness
      because the measured angular deflection now includes compliance from both the
      monocoque and the installed suspension structure.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The finalized ARG26 hub-to-hub stiffness used in this analysis was:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{hub}}
  =
  1907\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The hub-to-hub value is lower than the isolated monocoque value because adding
    suspension compliance increases the total angular deflection produced by the
    same applied torque.
  </p>

  <h3>Extracting Suspension Structural Stiffness</h3>

  <p>
    The hub-to-hub test does not directly measure the suspension structure by
    itself. Instead, it measures the monocoque and suspension structure acting
    together.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because the same torque passes through both compliant structures, their
    angular deflections add:
  </p>

  \[
  \theta_{\mathrm{hub}}
  =
  \theta_{\mathrm{mono}}
  +
  \theta_{\mathrm{susp}}
  \]

  <p>
    Using \(\theta=T/K\), this becomes the series-stiffness relationship:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{hub}}}
  =
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}}
  \]

  <p>
    Rearranging to isolate the suspension structural stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}}
  =
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{hub}}}
  -
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  \]

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}
  =
  \left(
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{hub}}}
  -
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
  \right)^{-1}
  \]

  <p>
    Substituting the two measured ARG26 stiffness values:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}
  =
  \left(
  \frac{1}{1907}
  -
  \frac{1}{3364}
  \right)^{-1}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}
  \approx
  4403\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    Therefore, the suspension structural stiffness was not measured as a separate
    direct test. It was extracted from the difference in compliance between the
    monocoque-only test and the complete hub-to-hub test.
  </p>

  <div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Test or calculated component</th>
          <th>Included compliance</th>
          <th>Torsional stiffness</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>

      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Monocoque-only test</td>
          <td>Composite monocoque and its mounting regions</td>
          <td>\(3364\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}\)</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
          <td>Hub-to-hub test</td>
          <td>Monocoque plus suspension structure</td>
          <td>\(1907\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}\)</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
          <td>Extracted suspension stiffness</td>
          <td>Suspension links, joints, mounts, rockers, and uprights</td>
          <td>\(4403\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}\)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    These measured values are then normalized by the equivalent torsional wheel
    rate and used in the following section to determine the minimum monocoque
    stiffness required for the next vehicle.
  </p>

</section>
</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Normalized Stiffness Values</h2>

  <p>
    To make the results independent of the absolute wheel rate, each stiffness
    is divided by the equivalent torsional wheel rate:
  </p>

  \[
  K_i'
  =
  \frac{K_i}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}}
  \]

  <p>
    By definition:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{wheel}}' = 1
  \]

  <h3>Normalized Monocoque Stiffness</h3>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}'
  =
  \frac{3364}{171.92}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}'
  \approx
  19.57
  }
  \]

  <h3>Normalized Suspension Structural Stiffness</h3>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
  =
  \frac{4403}{171.92}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
  \approx
  25.61
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The measured structural stiffness values were therefore:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}' = 19.57,
  \qquad
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}' = 25.61
  }
  \]

  <p>
    In physical terms, the monocoque was approximately 19.6 times as stiff as
    the equivalent wheel rate, while the suspension structure was approximately
    25.6 times as stiff.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Determining the Monocoque Stiffness Target</h2>

  <p>
    The ideal rigid-structure vehicle has no compliance in either the monocoque
    or the suspension structure. Its normalized stiffness is therefore
    controlled entirely by the wheel rate:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{vehicle,rigid}}' = 1
  \]

  <p>
    The target is for the actual vehicle to retain 90% of this ideal stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{vehicle,target}}' = 0.90
  \]

  <p>
    The normalized series-stiffness equation is:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{vehicle}}'}
  =
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}'}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}'}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}'}
  \]

  <p>
    Substituting the target vehicle stiffness, normalized wheel rate, and
    measured suspension structural stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{0.90}
  =
  1
  +
  \frac{1}{25.61}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
  \]

  <p>
    Rearranging:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
  =
  \frac{1}{0.90}
  -
  1
  -
  \frac{1}{25.61}
  \]

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  =
  \left(
  \frac{1}{0.90}
  -
  1
  -
  \frac{1}{25.61}
  \right)^{-1}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  \approx
  13.88
  }
  \]

  <p>
    Converting back to dimensional torsional stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  =
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  \]

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  =
  13.88
  \left(
  171.92\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  \right)
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  \approx
  2386\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Target vs. Measured</h2>

  <p>
    The calculated minimum monocoque target is approximately:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  \approx
  2390 \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  \]

  <p>
    The measured monocoque stiffness was:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,measured}}
  =
  3364\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  \]

  <p>
    The stiffness margin relative to the target is:
  </p>

  \[
  \text{Stiffness margin}
  =
  \frac{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,measured}}
  -
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  }{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  }
  \]

  \[
  \text{Stiffness margin}
  =
  \frac{3364-2386}{2386}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  \text{Stiffness margin}
  \approx
  41\%
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The measured monocoque was approximately 41% stiffer than the calculated
    minimum target. This indicates that the monocoque provides a sufficiently rigid platform
    for the suspension while avoiding the unnecessary mass associated with
    pursuing infinite structural stiffness.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Analysis of Margin of Error</h2>
  <p>
    This 41% difference between our targeted stiffness and our actual stiffness is because 
    our testing rig for our test panels had greater 
    compliance than expected. This resulted in needing to increase our ply schedules in 
    our composites in order to pass the structural equivalencey rules. 
    This lead to a heavier overall structure, and is why our stiffness was so much higher than expected.
    For this upcoming testing campaign, I am currently implementing and testing new rig designs,
    with the hope of reducing our ply schedules so we aren't stregnth limited, but instead right at our calculated stiffness target.  
  </p>

</section>

